1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,518
First, we write the operating system onto the microSD card.

2
00:00:04,518 --> 00:00:10,940
Raspberry Pi Imager copies a ready-to-run system onto the card, together with your settings:

3
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:14,430
the computer name, your user, your Wi-Fi, and SSH.

4
00:00:16,030 --> 00:00:20,000
Next, we slide the card into the slot under the Raspberry Pi.

5
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,839
Now plug in the power.

6
00:00:22,839 --> 00:00:28,139
The red light stays on, and the green light flickers while the Pi reads the card.

7
00:00:28,139 --> 00:00:30,429
The first boot takes a few minutes:

8
00:00:30,429 --> 00:00:34,158
the Pi resizes the file system and applies your settings.

9
00:00:34,158 --> 00:00:35,270
Do not unplug it.

10
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:42,510
Then the Pi joins your Wi-Fi network by itself, and receives its own address on the network.

11
00:00:43,910 --> 00:00:50,340
Finally, on your laptop, open PowerShell and type ssh, your user name, at raspi4.local.

12
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:53,591
Type yes the first time, then your password.

13
00:00:53,591 --> 00:00:55,882
Nothing appears while you type;

14
00:00:55,882 --> 00:00:56,991
that is normal.

15
00:00:56,991 --> 00:01:00,834
When you see the Pi's prompt, you are inside the Pi.

16
00:01:00,834 --> 00:01:04,086
Check its address with the hostname command.

17
00:01:04,086 --> 00:01:05,047
And remember:

18
00:01:05,047 --> 00:01:08,520
always shut down properly before you unplug it.
